Chapter 1 General Provisions

Article 1 (Purpose)
The purpose of this Decree is to prescribe matters delegated by the Special Act on Strengthening the Competitiveness of and Supporting the Semiconductor Industry and matters necessary for its implementation.

Article 2 (Areas Eligible for Designation as Semiconductor Clusters)
"Areas prescribed by Presidential Decree" in subparagraph 2 (c) of Article 2 of the Special Act on Strengthening the Competitiveness of and Supporting the Semiconductor Industry (hereinafter referred to as the "Act") means the following areas:
1. Free economic zones under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Special Act on Designation and Management of Free Economic Zones;
2. Specialized complexes for materials, components, and equipment under Article 45 (1) of the Act on Special Measures for Strengthening the Competitiveness of and Stabilizing Supply Chains for the Materials, Components, and Equipment Industries;
3. Special research and development zones under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Special Act on the Promotion of Special Research and Development Zones;
4. Special opportunity development zones under subparagraph 13 of Article 2 of the Special Act on Local Autonomy and Decentralization and Balanced Growth;
5. Other areas recognized and publicly notified by the Minister of Trade and Industry as suitable for clustering the semiconductor industry because institutions, enterprises, and facilities related to semiconductor design, manufacturing, supply, research, sales, support, etc. are concentrated there.

Chapter 2 Establishment of Master Plans for Strengthening Semiconductor Industry Competitiveness, etc.

Article 3 (Establishment of Master Plans for Strengthening Semiconductor Industry Competitiveness, etc.)
(1) "Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 5 (2) 12 of the Act means the following matters:
1. Matters concerning responses to supply and demand crises involving materials, components, equipment, etc. related to the semiconductor industry and the establishment of stockpiling and management systems;
2. Matters concerning prevention of the outflow abroad of technologies and personnel related to the semiconductor industry;
3. Matters concerning policies to promote private-sector investment in facilities and research and development for the semiconductor industry, and financial support;
4. Matters concerning technology development, establishment of a foundation for design, and creation of an industrial ecosystem to strengthen the competitiveness of the semiconductor industry.
(2) The Minister of Trade and Industry shall prepare guidelines for establishing sectoral plans, in consultation with the heads of relevant central administrative agencies, so that the heads of relevant central administrative agencies can efficiently establish the sectoral plans for their respective agencies under the first sentence of Article 5 (3) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "sectoral plans").
(3) The Minister of Trade and Industry shall notify the heads of relevant central administrative agencies of the guidelines for establishing sectoral plans prepared pursuant to paragraph (2).
(4) The heads of relevant central administrative agencies shall prepare sectoral plans for the fields under their jurisdiction in accordance with the guidelines for establishing sectoral plans notified pursuant to paragraph (3), and submit them to the Minister of Trade and Industry.
(5) Where the sectoral plans submitted pursuant to paragraph (4) overlap or conflict with each other, the Minister of Trade and Industry may request the heads of relevant central administrative agencies to revise, supplement, or adjust the sectoral plans.
(6) "Changes to minor matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the latter sentence of Article 5 (3) of the Act means any of the following cases:
1. Changes to matters that do not affect the purpose and direction of the master plan for strengthening semiconductor industry competitiveness under Article 5 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "master plan") and for which the grounds for change are clear;
2. Correction of calculation errors, typographical errors, omissions, or comparable obvious errors.
(7) The heads of relevant central administrative agencies shall endeavor to secure the financial resources necessary to implement the master plan.
(8) When the Minister of Trade and Industry establishes or modifies a master plan, the Minister shall publicly announce its contents on the website of the Ministry of Trade and Industry: Provided, That where there is concern that publication may impede national and economic security, growth of the national economy, or the strengthening of competitiveness and protection of the semiconductor industry, the Minister may publicly announce the master plan excluding the relevant portions.

Article 4 (Establishment and Implementation of Implementation Plans for Strengthening Semiconductor Industry Competitiveness, etc.)
(1) The heads of relevant central administrative agencies shall establish implementation plans for strengthening semiconductor industry competitiveness (hereinafter referred to as "implementation plans") pursuant to Article 6 (1) and (2) of the Act, and submit them by the last day of February each year to the Special Committee on Strengthening Semiconductor Industry Competitiveness under Article 9 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Committee").
(2) Implementation plans shall be finalized following deliberation by the Committee.
(3) Where necessary to ensure that the master plan and implementation plans are linked, the Minister of Trade and Industry may request the heads of relevant central administrative agencies to revise, supplement, or adjust their implementation plans before the Committee's deliberation under paragraph (2).
(4) When implementation plans submitted by the heads of relevant central administrative agencies have been finalized following deliberation by the Committee, the Minister of Trade and Industry shall consolidate them and publicly announce their contents on the website of the Ministry of Trade and Industry: Provided, That where there is concern that publication may impede national and economic security, growth of the national economy, or the strengthening of competitiveness and protection of the semiconductor industry, the Minister may publicly announce the implementation plans excluding the relevant portions.
(5) Where the heads of relevant central administrative agencies intend to change any of the following matters after implementation plans have been finalized, they shall do so following deliberation by the Committee:
1. Matters related to two or more central administrative agencies;
2. Other matters determined by the Committee that may have a significant impact on strengthening competitiveness and protecting the semiconductor industry.
(6) When an implementation plan is modified, the Minister of Trade and Industry shall publicly announce the contents of the modification on the website of the Ministry of Trade and Industry: Provided, That where there is concern that publication may impede national and economic security, growth of the national economy, or the strengthening of competitiveness and protection of the semiconductor industry, the Minister may publicly announce the modified contents excluding the relevant portions.
(7) The heads of relevant central administrative agencies shall submit the results of carrying out their implementation plans to the Committee by December 31 each year pursuant to Article 6 (2) of the Act.

Article 5 (Surveys of Current Conditions and Forecasting of Development Prospects of the Semiconductor Industry)
(1) The Minister of Trade and Industry and the heads of relevant central administrative agencies may commission institutions or organizations with expertise in the semiconductor industry to conduct surveys of current conditions and forecasts of development prospects of the semiconductor industry under Article 7 (1) of the Act.
(2) The Minister of Trade and Industry may request the heads of relevant central administrative agencies, heads of local governments, and heads of public institutions under Article 4 of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions (hereinafter referred to as "public institutions") that possess and manage information concerning the current conditions and development prospects of the semiconductor industry to submit the materials necessary for surveys of current conditions and forecasts of development prospects of the semiconductor industry under Article 7 (1) of the Act. In such cases, the head of an institution requested to submit materials shall comply unless there is a special reason not to do so.

Article 6 (Compilation and Management of Statistics Related to the Semiconductor Industry)
(1) The Minister of Trade and Industry and the heads of relevant central administrative agencies shall compile and manage statistics on the semiconductor industry under Article 8 (1) of the Act each year.
(2) The scope of statistics to be compiled on the semiconductor industry under Article 8 (1) of the Act shall be as follows:
1. Trends and prospects of domestic and overseas semiconductor markets;
2. Export and import trends of the semiconductor industry;
3. Trends in investment in facilities in the semiconductor industry;
4. Other matters deemed necessary by the Minister of Trade and Industry and the heads of relevant central administrative agencies to establish a foundation for strengthening semiconductor industry competitiveness and to systematically establish master plans and implementation plans.
(3) The Minister of Trade and Industry and the heads of relevant central administrative agencies may have the following institutions perform the work of compiling and managing statistics on the semiconductor industry pursuant to Article 8 (3) of the Act:
1. Nonprofit corporations established pursuant to Article 32 of the Civil Act for the purpose of performing work related to the semiconductor industry;
2. The Korea Institute for Advancement of Technology under Article 38 of the Industrial Technology Innovation Promotion Act and the Korea Planning and Evaluation Institute of Industrial Technology under Article 39 of the same Act;
3. The National IT Industry Promotion Agency under Article 26 of the Information and Communications Industry Promotion Act;
4. The Korea Institute for Industrial Economics and Trade under Article 8 of the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-Funded Research Institutions;
5. The National Research Foundation of Korea established pursuant to the National Research Foundation of Korea Act;
6. Other institutions recognized by the Minister of Trade and Industry and the heads of relevant central administrative agencies as having expertise in compiling and managing statistics related to the semiconductor industry.

Article 7 (Composition of the Committee, etc.)
(1) "Relevant central administrative agencies prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 9 (2) 1 of the Act means the following central administrative agencies:
1. The Ministry of Finance and Economy;
2. The Ministry of Science and ICT;
3. The Ministry of Education;
4. The Ministry of National Defense;
5. The Ministry of Trade and Industry;
6. The Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment;
7. The Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport;
8. The Ministry of SMEs and Startups;
9. The Ministry of Planning and Budget;
10. The Ministry of Intellectual Property;
11. The Financial Services Commission;
12. The Office for Government Policy Coordination;
13. Central administrative agencies related to matters placed on the Committee's agenda and designated by the chairperson of the Committee (hereinafter referred to as the "chairperson" in this Article through Article 10).
(2) The term of office of members commissioned pursuant to Article 9 (2) 2 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "commissioned members") shall be two years, and they may be reappointed consecutively only once: Provided, That the term of office of a newly commissioned member due to a member's resignation, etc. shall be the remainder of the predecessor's term.
(3) A commissioned member may continue to perform duties until a successor is commissioned, even after the term of office under paragraph (2) expires.
(4) Where the chairperson deems it necessary, the head of a relevant central administrative agency or a public official of equivalent rank who is not a member may attend a meeting of the Committee and speak.

Article 8 (Removal of Members)
The chairperson may remove a commissioned member if the member falls under any of the following subparagraphs:
1. Where the member becomes unable to perform duties for a prolonged period due to physical or mental infirmity, etc.;
2. Where the member has engaged in misconduct related to duties;
3. Where the member is deemed unsuitable to retain the position of member due to neglect of duties, impairment of dignity, or any other reason;
4. Where the member fails to recuse himself or herself despite falling under a ground for disqualification under any subparagraph of Article 9 (1);
5. Where the member violates or neglects the duty of confidentiality under Article 15;
6. Where the member voluntarily indicates that it is difficult to perform duties.

Article 9 (Disqualification, Challenge, and Recusal of Members)
(1) A member of the Committee who falls under any of the following subparagraphs shall be disqualified from the deliberation and resolution of the Committee:
1. Where the member or the member's spouse or former spouse is a party to the matter concerned (including an executive of the party where the party is a corporation, organization, etc.; hereinafter the same shall apply in this subparagraph and subparagraph 2), or is a joint holder of rights or a joint obligor with a party to the matter;
2. Where the member is or was a relative under Article 777 of the Civil Act of a party to the matter concerned;
3. Where the member has given testimony or a statement, provided advice, conducted research, performed services, or carried out an appraisal regarding the matter concerned;
4. Where the member or a corporation, organization, etc. to which the member belongs is or was an agent of a party to the matter concerned;
5. Where the member has served within the last three years as an executive or employee of a corporation that is a party to the matter concerned.
(2) Where a member has a ground for disqualification under any subparagraph of paragraph (1), or circumstances make it difficult to expect fair deliberation and resolution from the member, a party to the matter may file a challenge against the member with the Committee, and the Committee shall decide by resolution whether to accept the challenge. In such cases, the member subject to the challenge shall not participate in that resolution.
(3) Where a member falls under a ground for disqualification under any subparagraph of paragraph (1), the member shall voluntarily recuse himself or herself from the deliberation and resolution on the matter concerned.

Article 10 (Operation of the Committee, etc.)
(1) The chairperson shall represent the Committee and exercise overall control of its affairs.
(2) Where the chairperson is unable to perform duties due to unavoidable circumstances, a member designated in advance by the chairperson shall act on the chairperson's behalf.
(3) The chairperson shall convene meetings of the Committee and preside over them.
(4) A meeting of the Committee shall open with the attendance of a majority of the incumbent members (including members under Article 7 (1) 13), and resolutions shall be adopted with the affirmative votes of a majority of the members present: Provided, That where the chairperson deems it necessary, deliberation and resolution may be conducted in writing.
(5) Detailed matters necessary for the operation of the Committee, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (4), shall be determined by the chairperson following a resolution of the Committee.

Article 11 (Composition and Operation of Specialized Committees and Special Committees)
(1) Specialized committees for individual fields under Article 9 (4) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "specialized committees") shall be established separately for the necessary fields by the chairperson of the Committee, in consultation with the member serving as secretary under paragraph (3) of that Article.
(2) Each specialized committee and each special committee under the subparagraphs of Article 9 (4) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "special committees") shall consist of no more than 15 members, including one chairperson.
(3) Members of specialized committees and special committees shall be appointed or commissioned by the chairperson of the Committee from among the following persons, and the chairpersons of specialized committees and special committees shall each be designated by the chairperson of the Committee from among the members of the relevant specialized committee or special committee:
1. Members of the Committee;
2. Relevant public officials of central administrative agencies responsible for affairs related to the semiconductor industry;
3. Persons with extensive expertise and experience in the semiconductor industry;
4. Persons who belong to organizations or business associations related to the semiconductor industry or who are recommended by such organizations or associations.
(4) Meetings of specialized committees and special committees shall be convened by the chairperson of the relevant committee when requested by the chairperson of the Committee or when the chairperson of the relevant specialized committee or special committee deems it necessary, and the members of the relevant committee shall be informed in advance of the meeting plan, including the agenda, date, time, and place.
(5) The chairpersons of specialized committees and special committees shall report to the chairperson of the Committee the matters deliberated on by their respective committees and the results thereof.
(6) Article 9 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the disqualification, challenge, and recusal of members of specialized committees and special committees.
(7) Detailed matters necessary for the operation of specialized committees and special committees, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (6), shall be determined by the chairperson of the relevant specialized committee or special committee following a resolution of that committee.

Article 12 (Composition and Operation of the Coordination Committee)
(1) Pursuant to Article 9 (5) of the Act, the Committee may delegate the following matters to the Semiconductor Industry Coordination Committee (hereinafter referred to as the "Coordination Committee"):
1. Matters concerning the designation of semiconductor clusters, changes to or lifting of their designation, and support for semiconductor clusters under Articles 11 through 15 of the Act;
2. Matters concerning improvement of regulations relating to the semiconductor industry under Article 23 (1) of the Act;
3. Matters concerning expedited processing of authorizations, permits, etc. under Article 27 (2) of the Act;
4. Matters concerning the establishment, operation, and management of the Special Account for Strengthening Semiconductor Industry Competitiveness under Article 35 (1) of the Act;
5. Other matters that the Committee resolves require review, coordination, and consultation by the Coordination Committee.
(2) The Coordination Committee shall consist of no more than 20 members (hereinafter referred to as "Coordination Members"), including one chairperson of the Coordination Committee (hereinafter referred to as the "Coordination Chairperson").
(3) The following persons shall serve as Coordination Members. In such cases, where an agency has more than one vice minister, the vice minister designated by the head of that agency shall serve as a member.
1. The Vice Minister of Finance and Economy;
2. The Vice Minister of Science and ICT;
3. The Vice Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment;
4. The Vice Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport;
5. The Vice Minister of Planning and Budget;
6. The Vice Minister of Intellectual Property;
7. The Administrator of the Korea Heritage Service;
8. Vice ministers (including public officials of vice-ministerial rank) of central administrative agencies related to the agenda submitted to a meeting of the Coordination Committee and designated by the Coordination Chairperson;
9. Persons commissioned by the Coordination Chairperson from among experts in fields related to the agenda submitted to a meeting of the Coordination Committee.
(4) The Coordination Chairperson shall convene meetings of the Coordination Committee and preside over them.
(5) A meeting of the Coordination Committee shall be opened with the attendance of a majority of its incumbent members (including Coordination Members under paragraph (3) 8 and 9), and resolutions shall be passed with the affirmative vote of a majority of the members present.
(6) Articles 8 and 9 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the dismissal, disqualification, challenge, and recusal of Coordination Members.
(7) The Coordination Committee may request relevant central administrative agencies, relevant organizations, etc. to submit materials or opinions, or have relevant experts, interested parties, etc. attend meetings to hear their opinions.
(8) The Coordination Committee shall have one secretary for its efficient operation and support, and the secretary shall be designated by the Coordination Chairperson from among general-service public officials of the Ministry of Trade and Industry who belong to the Senior Civil Service.
(9) Detailed matters necessary for the operation of the Coordination Committee, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (2) through (8), shall be determined by the Coordination Chairperson following a resolution of the Coordination Committee.

Article 13 (Subcommittees of the Coordination Committee)
(1) The subcommittees established within the Coordination Committee pursuant to Article 9 (6) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "subcommittees") shall be as follows: Provided, That the Coordination Committee may establish additional subcommittees where necessary for the efficient conduct of deliberations.
1. The Semiconductor Cluster Subcommittee;
2. The Regulatory Improvement Subcommittee.
(2) Each subcommittee shall perform preliminary review, investigation, and analysis of the matters specified in the relevant subparagraph according to the following classifications: Provided, That a subcommittee additionally established pursuant to the proviso of paragraph (1), excluding its subparagraphs, shall perform preliminary review, investigation, and analysis of matters delegated by the Coordination Committee.
1. The Semiconductor Cluster Subcommittee:
(a) Matters concerning the designation of semiconductor clusters, changes to or lifting of their designation, and support for semiconductor clusters under Articles 11 through 15 of the Act;
(b) Other matters delegated by the Coordination Committee;
2. The Regulatory Improvement Subcommittee:
(a) Matters concerning improvement of regulations relating to the semiconductor industry under Article 23 (1) of the Act;
(b) Matters concerning expedited processing of authorizations, permits, etc. under Article 27 (2) of the Act;
(c) Other matters delegated by the Coordination Committee.
(3) The following persons shall serve as members of each subcommittee, and the chairperson of each subcommittee shall be designated by the chairperson of the Coordination Committee from among the members of the relevant subcommittee:
1. Public officials designated by the Coordination Chairperson from among public officials of the Ministry of Trade and Industry;
2. Public officials commissioned by the Coordination Chairperson from among public officials of relevant central administrative agencies;
3. Persons commissioned by the Coordination Chairperson from among experts in the field under the jurisdiction of the subcommittee.
(4) Article 11 (4) through (6) shall apply mutatis mutandis to the operation of subcommittees. In such cases, "specialized committees and special committees" shall be construed as "subcommittees," "chairpersons of specialized committees and special committees" as "chairpersons of subcommittees," and "chairperson of the Committee" as "Coordination Chairperson."
(5) Matters necessary for the composition and operation of subcommittees, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (3), shall be determined by the chairperson of the Coordination Committee following a resolution of the Coordination Committee.

Article 14 (Requests for Cooperation from Relevant Agencies)
(1) Where necessary for performing their duties, the Committee, specialized committees, special committees, the Coordination Committee, and subcommittees may commission relevant experts, public institutions, research institutes, or other agencies, organizations, etc. to conduct investigations or research.
(2) Where necessary for performing their duties, the Committee, specialized committees, special committees, the Coordination Committee, and subcommittees may gather opinions through surveys, public hearings, seminars, or other means.

Article 15 (Duty of Confidentiality)
Members of the Committee, specialized committees, special committees, the Coordination Committee, and subcommittees shall neither disclose secrets learned in the course of their duties nor use them for purposes other than their duties.

Article 16 (Composition and Operation of the Semiconductor Innovation and Growth Support Unit)
(1) The Semiconductor Innovation and Growth Support Unit under Article 10 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Support Unit") shall have one head, who shall be designated by the Minister of Trade and Industry from among general-service public officials of the Ministry of Trade and Industry who belong to the Senior Civil Service.
(2) The head of the Support Unit shall exercise overall control of the affairs of the Support Unit under the direction of the Minister of Trade and Industry.
(3) Matters necessary for the composition and operation of the Support Unit, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) and (2), shall be determined by the Minister of Trade and Industry.

CHAPTER III DESIGNATION AND SUPPORT OF SEMICONDUCTOR CLUSTERS

Article 17 (Procedure for Designation of Semiconductor Clusters)
(1) The term "matters prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as development of sites for semiconductor clusters and directions for their development" in Article 11 (3) of the Act means the following matters:
1. Basic objectives and medium- and long-term directions for the development of the semiconductor cluster;
2. The name, location, and area of the semiconductor cluster;
3. Matters concerning the project implementer intending to develop the semiconductor cluster and the method of project implementation;
4. The current status of the semiconductor industry and related infrastructure in the area where the semiconductor cluster is to be developed;
5. A land use plan for development of the site of the semiconductor cluster and a plan for installation of major infrastructure;
6. A plan for concentrating semiconductor industries, including the proportions and layout of business types related to the semiconductor industry;
7. Matters concerning training of personnel and establishment of a research base for the semiconductor industry;
8. Matters concerning securing financial resources to pursue the strengthening of competitiveness of the semiconductor industry;
9. Matters concerning environmental conservation and pollution prevention;
10. Other matters that the Minister of Trade and Industry deems necessary for development of semiconductor clusters and publicly notifies.
(2) When preparing a semiconductor cluster development plan under Article 11 (3) of the Act, a person intending to apply for designation of a semiconductor cluster shall consult in advance with the heads of local governments, business operators, institutions, etc. related to the semiconductor cluster for which designation is sought.
(3) Where a business operator engaged in the semiconductor industry applies to the Minister of Trade and Industry for designation of a semiconductor cluster, accompanied by a semiconductor cluster development plan pursuant to Article 11 (3) of the Act, the operator shall, pursuant to Article 11 (4) of the Act, be deemed to have applied for designation as the project implementer of the semiconductor cluster under paragraph (3) of that Article (hereinafter referred to as the "project implementer").
(4) The term "requirements prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 11 (5) 4 of the Act means being capable of contributing to balanced national development and reducing industrial disparities between regions.
(5) When designating a semiconductor cluster pursuant to Article 11 (6) of the Act, the Minister of Trade and Industry shall give priority consideration to areas outside the Seoul Metropolitan Area (meaning the Seoul Metropolitan Area under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Seoul Metropolitan Area Readjustment Planning Act; hereinafter the same shall apply).
(6) Where the Minister of Trade and Industry approves a semiconductor cluster development plan and designates a semiconductor cluster, the Minister shall publicly announce the details on the website of the Ministry of Trade and Industry.
(7) Matters necessary for designation of semiconductor clusters, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (6), shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Trade and Industry.

Article 18 (Changes to and Lifting of Semiconductor Cluster Designation)
(1) The term "where intending to change minor matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the proviso of Article 12 (1) of the Act, excluding its subparagraphs, means any of the following cases:
1. Where intending to change the name of a semiconductor cluster;
2. Where intending to change the total area designated as a semiconductor cluster within a range of 10 percent;
3. Where intending to change the name or address of the project implementer;
4. Where intending to correct a lot number, land category, or area due to a simple mistake, measurement error, etc.;
5. Where intending to change terminology, etc. following the enactment, amendment, or repeal of other statutes or regulations;
6. Where intending to change other matters that do not materially affect the purpose of designation and for which the grounds for the change are clear.
(2) Where the Minister of Trade and Industry intends to change or lift the designation of a semiconductor cluster pursuant to the main clause of Article 12 (1) of the Act, the Minister shall hear the opinion of the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, Integrated Metropolitan City Mayor, Metropolitan City Mayor, Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Do Governor, or Special Self-Governing Province Governor having jurisdiction over the relevant semiconductor cluster (hereinafter referred to as a "Mayor/Do Governor") before deliberation by the Committee.
(3) Where the Minister of Trade and Industry changes or lifts the designation of a semiconductor cluster pursuant to Article 12 (1) of the Act, the Minister shall publicly announce the matters specified in the following classifications on the website of the Ministry of Trade and Industry and notify the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and the Mayor/Do Governor having jurisdiction without delay:
1. Where the designation of a semiconductor cluster is changed: The matters changed;
2. Where the designation of a semiconductor cluster is lifted: The following matters:
(a) The name and location of the semiconductor cluster whose designation is lifted;
(b) The date of designation and the date of lifting of designation of the semiconductor cluster;
(c) The grounds for lifting the designation of the semiconductor cluster.
(4) Matters necessary for changes to or lifting of the designation of semiconductor clusters, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (3), shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Trade and Industry.

Article 19 (Policies for Fostering Semiconductor Clusters)
The term "matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 13 (2) 6 of the Act means the following matters:
1. Matters concerning securing and training specialized personnel in the semiconductor industry needed for semiconductor clusters and creating conditions conducive to their settlement;
2. Matters concerning joint research and development, information exchange, and establishment of a cooperation system among companies located in semiconductor clusters, research institutes, and support institutions.

Article 20 (Support for Development and Operation of Industrial Infrastructure in Semiconductor Clusters)
(1) The term "facilities prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 14 (1) 1 of the Act means electricity supply facilities that supply electricity to semiconductor clusters.
(2) The term "facilities prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 14 (1) 2 of the Act means water supply facilities that supply industrial water and domestic water to semiconductor clusters.
(3) The term "facilities prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 14 (1) 3 of the Act means sewerage systems, public wastewater treatment facilities, and waste treatment facilities that treat wastewater and waste generated in semiconductor clusters.
(4) The term "roads prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 14 (1) 4 of the Act means roads used for the development and operation of semiconductor clusters.
(5) The term "facilities prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 14 (1) 5 of the Act means the following facilities:
1. Gas facilities, telecommunications facilities, district energy supply facilities, and utility tunnels;
2. Redundant facilities under Article 18 (1) of the Act;
3. Other safety and public facilities necessary for the smooth development and operation of semiconductor clusters that are determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Trade and Industry.
(6) To support the development and operation of industrial infrastructure under Article 14 (1) of the Act, the proportion of the total costs borne by the State and local governments shall be a ratio determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Trade and Industry within a range of at least 50 percent and no more than 100 percent of the total project cost for each facility (including design, construction, and construction supervision costs, and other costs incurred in connection with implementation of the relevant project): Provided, That the full costs may be supported in any of the following cases. In such cases, details concerning the scope and ratio of support for the development and operation of industrial infrastructure shall be publicly notified by the Minister of Trade and Industry in consultation with the Minister of Planning and Budget, after hearing the opinion of the Mayor/Do Governor having jurisdiction over the relevant semiconductor cluster.
1. Where redundant facilities under Article 18 (1) of the Act are installed or expanded;
2. Where the industrial infrastructure is for common use within a semiconductor cluster and contributes to supply chain stability and industrial safety;
3. Where the industrial infrastructure of a semiconductor cluster contributes to balanced national development and reducing industrial disparities between regions;
4. Where the industrial infrastructure is necessary for the development and operation of a semiconductor cluster in which small and medium enterprises (meaning small and medium enterprises under Article 2 (1) of the Framework Act on Small and Medium Enterprises; hereinafter referred to as "SMEs") and middle-standing enterprises (meaning middle-standing enterprises under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Special Act on the Promotion of Growth and the Strengthening of Competitiveness of Middle-Standing Enterprises; hereinafter the same shall apply) occupy at least 30 percent of the total area or constitute at least 30 percent of the total number of resident enterprises;
5. Where the Committee determines that the industrial infrastructure needs to be developed and operated in the relevant semiconductor cluster, taking into account the degree of its contribution to national and economic security and the development of the national economy;
6. Other cases where the Minister of Trade and Industry deems it necessary to strengthen the competitiveness of the semiconductor industry and ensure stable operation of semiconductor clusters.
(7) Where deemed necessary in consideration of fiscal conditions, the importance of the relevant industrial infrastructure and the need for its prompt development, the appropriateness of the sharing of financial burdens between the State and local governments, etc., the Minister of Trade and Industry may adjust the ratio of support for the costs of developing and operating industrial infrastructure, notwithstanding paragraph (6). In such cases, the ratio shall be publicly notified by the Minister of Trade and Industry in consultation with the Minister of Planning and Budget, after hearing the opinion of the Mayor/Do Governor having jurisdiction over the relevant semiconductor cluster.
(8) The heads of relevant central administrative agencies and the heads of local governments shall cooperate to ensure that procedures for authorizations, permits, etc. under relevant statutes or regulations are handled promptly and efficiently for the prompt development of industrial infrastructure under paragraphs (1) through (5), and the Minister of Trade and Industry may request the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and the heads of local governments to take measures necessary for the prompt development of industrial infrastructure, such as priority processing of authorizations, permits, etc.
(9) The term "enterprises prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as enterprises that have entered into occupancy contracts" in Article 14 (2) of the Act means enterprises falling under any of the following subparagraphs:
1. Enterprises that have entered into occupancy contracts under Article 38 of the Industrial Cluster Development and Factory Establishment Act;
2. Enterprises that have entered into sales contracts or lease contracts for sites in semiconductor clusters;
3. Enterprises that have entered into investment agreements or other such agreements with the State or a local government and have established specific investment plans in order to locate in semiconductor clusters.

Article 21 (Support for Companies and Institutions, etc. Located in Semiconductor Clusters)
(1) When providing financial and administrative support under Article 15(1) of the Act, the State and local governments shall consider its contribution to balanced national development and the reduction of industrial disparities between regions, and shall give priority to areas outside the Seoul Metropolitan Area when considering the recipients of such support.
(2) The rate of reduction of or exemption from usage fees and rent for state property under Article 15(4) of the Act shall be determined, within the range of 50 percent to 100 percent of the usage fees and rent for the relevant state property, by the head of the central government office responsible for that state property (including any person to whom authority has been delegated or entrusted pursuant to Articles 28, 29, and 42(1) and (4) of the State Property Act; hereinafter the same shall apply in this Article), in consultation with the Minister of Trade and Industry.
(3) A person seeking a reduction of or exemption from usage fees and rent for state or public property pursuant to Article 15(4) of the Act shall apply for such reduction or exemption as follows:
1. Where seeking a reduction of or exemption from usage fees and rent for state property: Apply to the head of the central government office responsible for the relevant state property;
2. Where seeking a reduction of or exemption from usage fees and rent for public property: Apply to the head of the relevant local government as prescribed by ordinance of that local government.
(4) The cases in which the State and local governments may purchase land designated as a semiconductor cluster pursuant to the former part of Article 15(6) of the Act shall be as follows:
1. Where they intend to support innovative development under Article 17 of the Act by supplying SMEs and middle-standing enterprises with sites for lease at low cost;
2. Where it is necessary for the State and local governments to take the lead in securing sites to promptly establish an innovation ecosystem for strengthening semiconductor industry competitiveness;
3. Where it is necessary for the State and local governments to take the lead in securing sites to concentrate facilities for technology development and workforce training, such as semiconductor-related research facilities and universities.
(5) “Reshoring enterprises prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 15(7) of the Act means enterprises selected as reshoring enterprises eligible for support pursuant to Article 7 of the Act on Assistance to Korean Offshore Enterprises in Repatriation.
(6) Where the State provides funding to a local government pursuant to Article 15(9) of the Act, it shall consider the financial circumstances of the local government, the scale of development of the semiconductor cluster, and other relevant factors. In such cases, the standard subsidy rate under Article 4 of the Enforcement Decree of the Subsidy Management Act shall apply to the national subsidy rate, but differentiated support may be provided in accordance with the increased subsidy rate under Article 5 of the same Decree.
(7) Matters necessary for procedures for applying for support, criteria and methods for support, follow-up management, and other matters concerning support for companies and institutions, etc. located in semiconductor clusters, other than those provided for in paragraphs (1) through (6), shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Trade and Industry.

CHAPTER IV SUPPORT FOR THE SEMICONDUCTOR INDUSTRY, ETC.

Article 22 (Support for Innovative Development of SMEs, etc.)
“Support prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 17, subparagraph 8 of the Act means the following support:
1. Support for improving living conditions, including work, housing, education, healthcare, and culture, within semiconductor clusters developed in areas outside the Seoul Metropolitan Area;
2. Investment funding to strengthen the capabilities of SMEs and middle-standing enterprises constituting the semiconductor industry ecosystem, including investment in facilities, performance upgrades, and transition to the commercialization stage, and fiscal and financial assistance linked thereto;
3. Other support that the Minister of Trade and Industry and the heads of relevant central administrative agencies deem necessary for the innovative development of SMEs and middle-standing enterprises engaged in the semiconductor industry.

Article 23 (Installation and Expansion of Industrial Infrastructure)
(1) The scope of installation and expansion of redundant facilities under Article 18(1) of the Act shall be as follows:
1. For electricity networks: Installation and expansion of backup transmission lines and backup substations to ensure that the electricity supply is not interrupted even if an accident or malfunction occurs in a single transmission line or substation;
2. For water supply networks: Installation and expansion of alternative water sources, such as backup water storage facilities, and backup pipelines to ensure that the industrial water supply is not interrupted even if an accident or malfunction occurs at a water intake source or in a pipeline;
3. For roads: Installation and expansion of auxiliary access roads, bypass roads, connecting roads, emergency transport roads, and their ancillary facilities to ensure that the transportation of goods and vehicle traffic necessary for the operation of semiconductor industry facilities are not interrupted even if an accident or malfunction occurs on a major road, bridge, tunnel, or similar facility;
4. For other facilities: Facilities that the Minister of Trade and Industry deems necessary to prevent critical losses in production processes in the event of an accident and specifies by public notice.
(2) Where matters concerning the installation or expansion of industrial infrastructure need to be reflected, pursuant to Article 18(1) of the Act, in the master plan for electricity supply and demand under Article 25 of the Electric Utility Act, the national master plan for water supply under Article 4 of the Water Supply and Waterworks Installation Act, and the road construction and management plan under Article 6 of the Road Act (hereinafter referred to as “electricity supply and demand plans, etc.”), the Minister of Trade and Industry may submit a proposal requesting the inclusion of industrial infrastructure in electricity supply and demand plans, etc., containing the following matters, to the heads of the relevant central administrative agencies responsible for electricity supply and demand plans, etc. (including road management authorities under Article 2, subparagraph 5 of the Road Act that formulate road construction and management plans pursuant to Article 6 of the same Act; hereinafter the same shall apply in this Article):
1. Matters concerning anticipated electricity and water demand and road traffic volumes for each semiconductor cluster and major place of business;
2. Matters concerning the installation and expansion of redundant facilities under Article 18(1) of the Act;
3. Other matters that need to be reflected in electricity supply and demand plans, etc. for the installation and expansion of industrial infrastructure.
(3) The heads of the relevant central administrative agencies who receive a proposal requesting the inclusion of industrial infrastructure in electricity supply and demand plans, etc. pursuant to paragraph (2) shall review the proposal and reflect matters concerning the installation and expansion of electricity, water supply, and road networks necessary for the smooth provision of industrial infrastructure in the respective electricity supply and demand plans, etc. under their jurisdiction.
(4) The Minister of Trade and Industry shall inspect, at least once each year, whether the matters submitted pursuant to paragraph (2) have been reflected in electricity supply and demand plans, etc. and the status of installation of the relevant industrial infrastructure, and shall report the results to the Committee. In such cases, where the Minister of Trade and Industry deems, as a result of the inspection, that supplementation is necessary, the Minister may request supplementation from the heads of the relevant central administrative agencies before reporting to the Committee.
(5) An authority empowered to decide on urban or Gun management plans pursuant to Article 29 of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act shall cooperate to ensure that administrative procedures relating to the installation and expansion of the relevant industrial infrastructure, including decisions on urban or Gun management plans containing matters concerning the installation and expansion of industrial infrastructure reflected in electricity supply and demand plans, etc. pursuant to paragraph (3), are handled promptly and efficiently.

Article 24 (Support for the Semiconductor Contract Manufacturing Industry, etc.)
(1) The recipients of support under the development measures referred to in Article 19(1) of the Act shall be business operators engaged in the semiconductor industries specified in Article 2, subparagraph 1(b) and (c) of the Act.
(2) The development measures under Article 19(1) of the Act shall include the following matters concerning the business operators under paragraph (1):
1. Matters concerning support for investment in equipment and the establishment of research and demonstration infrastructure to advance and upgrade semiconductor contract manufacturing and back-end manufacturing processes;
2. Matters concerning support for the establishment of cooperative arrangements and joint development between semiconductor contract manufacturing companies and materials, components, and equipment companies;
3. Matters concerning support for the development and demonstration of the following semiconductor-related technologies, the establishment of infrastructure, pilot adoption by public institutions, the purchase of prototypes, and commercialization:
(a) Advanced packaging technologies;
(b) Contract manufacturing technologies for key materials, components, and equipment and system semiconductors;
4. Matters concerning support for the training of specialized personnel, the strengthening of the capabilities of incumbent workers, and employment stability in the semiconductor contract manufacturing and back-end processing fields;
5. Matters concerning support for the development of overseas sales channels, responses to international standardization, and technological cooperation to expand participation in international supply chains;
6. Other matters for which the Minister of Trade and Industry deems support necessary to strengthen the competitiveness of the semiconductor contract manufacturing and back-end processing industries.

Article 25 (Deliberation on Applications for Regulatory Improvement, etc.)
(1) When deliberating on the contents of an application for regulatory improvement, the findings of reviews by relevant administrative agencies, the results of processing the application, whether to improve the regulation, and other matters pursuant to the former part of Article 23(4) of the Act, the Committee shall comprehensively consider the following matters:
1. The specificity and feasibility of the contents of the application for regulatory improvement;
2. The possibility of achieving the intended purpose through regulatory improvement;
3. The broader effects resulting from the relevant regulatory improvement;
4. Other matters that the chairperson of the Committee deems necessary for reviewing the relevant regulatory improvement.
(2) Where the head of a relevant administrative agency commences or completes the revision of relevant statutes pursuant to Article 23(6) of the Act, or grants a regulatory exception pursuant to paragraph (7) of the same Article, the head shall notify the Committee of that fact.

Article 26 (Special Cases Concerning Preliminary Feasibility Studies for Projects of Public Enterprises and Quasi-Governmental Institutions)
(1) The head of a public enterprise or quasi-governmental institution seeking to apply for exemption from a preliminary feasibility study pursuant to Article 25(1) of the Act shall submit an application containing the following matters to the Minister of Finance and Economy:
1. The purpose and necessity of the project;
2. The scale of the project, its implementation structure, and its financing plan;
3. The reasons why exemption from a preliminary feasibility study is necessary and materials substantiating those reasons;
4. The results of the Committee’s deliberation and resolution.
(2) Where the Minister of Finance and Economy receives an application for exemption from a preliminary feasibility study under paragraph (1), the Minister shall notify the head of the relevant public enterprise or quasi-governmental institution of whether exemption is granted within 30 days: Provided, That where notification within 30 days is difficult for unavoidable reasons, the notification period may be extended only once by up to 15 days.

Article 27 (Special Cases Concerning Expedited Processing of Authorizations, Permits, etc.)
(1) A person seeking to apply for expedited processing of authorizations, permits, etc. pursuant to Article 27(1) of the Act shall submit an application form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Trade and Industry to the Minister of Trade and Industry, accompanied by the following documents:
1. Materials submitted, when applying for the authorizations, permits, etc., to the head of the administrative agency empowered to grant the relevant authorizations, permits, etc. (hereinafter referred to as the “authorization and permit authority”);
2. Reply materials received from the authorization and permit authority after the application for authorizations, permits, etc.;
3. Materials substantiating the requested period for expedited processing of authorizations, permits, etc.;
4. Materials demonstrating that delays in processing authorizations, permits, etc. are likely to significantly impede the development and operation of a semiconductor cluster;
5. Other materials explaining the necessity for expedited processing of authorizations, permits, etc.
(2) When hearing the opinion of the relevant authorization and permit authority pursuant to the latter part of Article 27(2) of the Act, the Minister of Trade and Industry may request that authority to submit its opinion on the application for expedited processing of authorizations, permits, etc., specifying a period of up to 15 days: Provided, That where the relevant authorization and permit authority requests an extension of the period for unavoidable reasons, such as a natural disaster, the period may be extended by up to 15 days.
(3) An authorization and permit authority requested to submit its opinion pursuant to paragraph (2) shall submit its opinion within the submission period. In such cases, if no opinion is submitted within that period, the authority shall be deemed to have no opinion.
(4) When deliberating and resolving on expedited processing of authorizations, permits, etc. pursuant to Article 27(2) of the Act, the Committee shall consider the following matters:
1. The urgency and economic effects of the development and operation of the semiconductor cluster;
2. The reasons for delays in authorizations, permits, etc. and the feasibility of expedited processing;
3. Whether the standards under relevant statutes are met and the effects on the environment, safety, and other relevant matters.
(5) “Special reasons prescribed by Presidential Decree” in the latter part of Article 27(2) of the Act means any of the following cases:
1. Where a war, a natural disaster under the Countermeasures against Natural Disasters Act, a Class 1 infectious disease under the Infectious Disease Control and Prevention Act, or another comparable disaster or accident occurs;
2. Where the development and operation of a semiconductor cluster become difficult because a project implementer is subject to a decision to commence rehabilitation proceedings or a declaration of bankruptcy under the Debtor Rehabilitation and Bankruptcy Act, or commits an unlawful act;
3. Where it is difficult for the authorization and permit authority to formulate a plan for processing authorizations, permits, etc. because the project implementer fails, without justifiable grounds, to comply with a request by that authority to supplement materials under the main clause of Article 27(3) of the Act;
4. Other cases in which the Committee recognizes that there are compelling reasons of public interest that make a delay in processing authorizations, permits, etc. unavoidable.
(6) Notification under Article 27(4) of the Act shall be made using a results notification form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Trade and Industry.

Article 28 (Supply Chain Internalization and Operation of Testbeds)
(1) The Minister of Trade and Industry and the heads of relevant central administrative agencies may provide funding for the following items for research and development conducted jointly through collaboration between domestic materials, components, and equipment companies and system semiconductor companies and domestic user companies pursuant to Article 29(1)1 of the Act:
1. The purchase cost of materials, components, and equipment and rent for research and development facilities;
2. Personnel expenses for research and development personnel and expenses for research and development activities;
3. Expenses incurred in the production and performance evaluation of prototypes for commercialization, and other related expenses.
(2) Pursuant to Article 29(1)2 of the Act, the Minister of Trade and Industry may designate the facilities and equipment infrastructure held by the following institutions as testbeds (meaning facilities and equipment infrastructure necessary for demonstration testing, reliability assessment, performance verification, etc.; hereinafter the same shall apply) and have the relevant institutions operate those testbeds:
1. Institutions that utilize semiconductor-related technologies or operate semiconductor-related facilities among the following institutions:
(a) Public institutions;
(b) Government-funded research institutes in the fields of science and technology under Article 2 of the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-Funded Science and Technology Research Institutes;
(c) Government-funded research institutes under Article 2 of the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-Funded Research Institutes;
(d) Specific research institutes under Article 2 of the Specific Research Institutes Support Act;
(e) Schools under Article 2 of the Higher Education Act;
2. Private companies and research institutes that possess semiconductor manufacturing facilities and are capable of actual mass production of materials, components, and equipment;
3. Other institutions that the Minister of Trade and Industry recognizes as having facilities and equipment infrastructure suitable for verifying the performance of products developed by domestic materials, components, and equipment companies and system semiconductor companies.
(3) When designating a testbed, the Minister of Trade and Industry shall comprehensively consider the following matters:
1. Possession of facilities and equipment necessary for demonstration testing, reliability assessment, performance verification, etc. (hereinafter referred to as “demonstration testing, etc.”), and the capacity to operate them;
2. The current status of securing dedicated specialized personnel for demonstration testing, etc.;
3. The current status of, and plans for, establishing arrangements for links with domestic user companies and security management systems for the results of demonstration testing, etc.;
4. Plans for the purchase and use of system semiconductors and equipment and systems utilizing them for demonstration testing, etc.
(4) Where an institution operating a testbed purchases any of the following products from an SME or a middle-standing enterprise for demonstration testing, etc., the Minister of Trade and Industry and the heads of relevant central administrative agencies may provide a subsidy of up to 50 percent of the purchase cost:
1. Semiconductor materials, components, and equipment;
2. System semiconductors.
(5) Necessary details concerning the methods and procedures for pursuing the matters in each subparagraph of Article 29(1) of the Act, other than those provided for in paragraphs (1) through (4), shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Trade and Industry and the heads of relevant central administrative agencies.

Article 29 (Identification, Attraction, and Support of Outstanding Personnel from Overseas)
(1) The Minister of Trade and Industry may use the results obtained through surveys and analyses concerning specialists at overseas universities, research institutes, and enterprises pursuant to Article 30(2) of the Act for the following projects:
1. Projects to identify and attract outstanding personnel from overseas in connection with the semiconductor industry;
2. International exchange projects, including education through dispatch to overseas institutions and participation in training, to foster outstanding domestic personnel in connection with the semiconductor industry;
3. Projects to establish and manage a database (meaning a collection of information systematically organized and processed to enable users to search and use it) on outstanding personnel from overseas in connection with the semiconductor industry;
4. Other projects the Minister of Trade and Industry deems necessary to attract outstanding personnel from overseas in connection with the semiconductor industry.
(2) The Minister of Trade and Industry and the heads of relevant central administrative agencies may provide the following administrative and financial support to identify and attract outstanding personnel from overseas pursuant to Article 30(5) of the Act:
1. Support for expenses necessary for enterprises, research institutes, specialized institutions, universities, business associations, etc. to identify and attract outstanding personnel from overseas and establish international networks;
2. Support for expenses of surveys and research on the current state and outlook of demand for and supply of outstanding personnel from overseas at enterprises, research institutes, specialized institutions, universities, business associations, etc.;
3. Facilitation of entry into and departure from the Republic of Korea by outstanding personnel from overseas and support for simplifying visa issuance procedures;
4. Collection and provision of employment information, including job vacancies and job searches, concerning outstanding personnel from overseas;
5. Support for outstanding personnel from overseas and their families in adapting to life in the Republic of Korea, securing stable housing, and settling in the Republic of Korea;
6. Other matters the Minister of Trade and Industry and the heads of relevant central administrative agencies deem necessary to identify and attract outstanding personnel from overseas.
(3) The Minister of Trade and Industry and the heads of relevant central administrative agencies may give priority in providing the support under paragraph (2)3 and 5 to outstanding personnel from overseas who are employed or conduct research at semiconductor clusters or workplaces related to the semiconductor industry located outside the Seoul Metropolitan Area.

CHAPTER V SUPPORT FOR HUMAN RESOURCES DEVELOPMENT IN THE SEMICONDUCTOR INDUSTRY

Article 30 (Development and Securing of Human Resources for the Semiconductor Industry)
When implementing the matters set forth in the subparagraphs of Article 32(2) of the Act, the Minister of Trade and Industry may give priority to projects for linking semiconductor industry enterprises located outside the Seoul Metropolitan Area with the employment of specialists in the relevant regions and for retraining such specialists.

Article 31 (Designation of Institutions for Training Specialists for the Semiconductor Industry)
(1) "Criteria prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 33(1) of the Act means the following criteria:
1. The objectives of the education and training project shall be clear, and its project plan shall be feasible;
2. The curriculum and educational content shall be appropriate for training the specialists necessary for the development of the semiconductor industry;
3. A plan shall have been established to procure the funding necessary for the education and training project;
4. The educational facilities and equipment necessary for the education and training project shall be possessed;
5. Specialized teaching personnel and dedicated operational personnel necessary for the education and training project shall have been secured.
(2) An institution, organization, etc. seeking designation as an institution for training specialists for the semiconductor industry under Article 33(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "training institution") shall submit to the Minister of Trade and Industry an application prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Trade and Industry, accompanied by the following documents:
1. A project plan including a plan to procure the funding necessary for the education and training project;
2. Articles of incorporation (applicable only to a corporation);
3. Documents proving compliance with the criteria under paragraph (1).
(3) Upon receiving an application under paragraph (2), the Minister of Trade and Industry shall examine whether the applicant meets the criteria set forth in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) and possesses expertise in education and training, and determine whether to grant designation. Where the applicant is designated as a training institution, the Minister shall issue a certificate of designation prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Trade and Industry.
(4) Where the Minister of Trade and Industry designates a training institution pursuant to paragraph (3), the Minister shall publicly announce the designation on the website of the Ministry of Trade and Industry.
(5) The Minister of Trade and Industry may provide a training institution with support for the following expenses pursuant to Article 33(2) of the Act:
1. Expenses for developing and operating education and training programs;
2. Expenses for purchasing, maintaining, and repairing practical training equipment;
3. Personnel and training expenses for specialized teaching personnel and dedicated operational personnel;
4. Other expenses for which the Minister of Trade and Industry deems support necessary to train specialists for the semiconductor industry.
(6) When designating training institutions, the Minister of Trade and Industry shall endeavor to ensure that institutions located outside the Seoul Metropolitan Area account for at least 50 percent of the total number of designated training institutions.
(7) Matters necessary for the designation and operation of training institutions, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (6), shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Trade and Industry.

Article 32 (Designation of and Support for Universities Specializing in Semiconductors, etc.)
(1) The Minister of Trade and Industry may, in consultation with the Minister of Education, designate the institutions set forth in the subparagraphs of Article 34(1) of the Act as universities specializing in semiconductors, specialized graduate schools, or high schools tailored to demand from the semiconductor industry (hereinafter referred to as "universities specializing in semiconductors, etc.") pursuant to that paragraph. In such cases, where the Minister intends to designate an institution under Article 34(1)2 through 5 of the Act as a university specializing in semiconductors, etc., the Minister shall also consult with the Minister of Science and ICT.
(2) A person seeking designation as a university specializing in semiconductors, etc. pursuant to Article 34(1) of the Act shall submit to the Minister of Trade and Industry an application prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Trade and Industry.
(3) Where the Minister of Trade and Industry designates a university specializing in semiconductors, etc. pursuant to Article 34(1) of the Act, the Minister shall publicly announce its name, location, purpose of designation, etc. on the website of the Ministry of Trade and Industry.
(4) Expenses eligible for support under Article 34(2) of the Act shall be as follows:
1. Expenses for developing and operating curricula for training specialists for the semiconductor industry;
2. Expenses for basic and applied research and industry-academia collaborative research relating to semiconductors;
3. Expenses for increasing the number of specialized teaching staff in the semiconductor field and utilizing experts from the semiconductor industry as teaching personnel;
4. Expenses for establishing advanced equipment and facilities for practical training relating to semiconductors;
5. Expenses for developing campuses and maintaining and repairing facilities of universities that have relocated to semiconductor clusters pursuant to Article 32(2)8 of the Act.
(5) When designating universities specializing in semiconductors, etc., the Minister of Trade and Industry may give priority consideration to universities and high schools located outside the Seoul Metropolitan Area.
(6) Matters necessary for the detailed designation criteria, evaluation methods, and designation procedures for universities specializing in semiconductors, etc., other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (5), shall be publicly notified by the Minister of Trade and Industry in consultation with the Minister of Education.

Article 33 (Establishment and Operation of Departments and Schools by Contract, etc.)
(1) The head of an industrial educational institution under Article 2, subparagraph 2 of the Industrial Education Enhancement and Industry-Academia-Research Cooperation Promotion Act who seeks support pursuant to Article 34(3) of the Act for the expenses necessary for establishing and operating departments and schools established under a contract pursuant to Article 8 of the former Act with a business operator engaged in the semiconductor industry (hereinafter referred to as "contract departments, etc.") shall submit to the Minister of Trade and Industry or the head of the relevant central administrative agency an application prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Trade and Industry.
(2) Upon receiving an application under paragraph (1), the Minister of Trade and Industry or the head of the relevant central administrative agency may provide the head of the relevant industrial educational institution with support for all or part of the following expenses within budgetary limits pursuant to Article 34(3) of the Act:
1. Contributions necessary for the operation of contract departments, etc.;
2. Tuition fees for students enrolled in contract departments, etc.;
3. Expenses for establishing advanced equipment and facilities for practical training relating to semiconductors;
4. Expenses for developing curricula for contract departments, etc. and operating industry-academia cooperation programs.
(3) When providing support pursuant to paragraph (2), the Minister of Trade and Industry and the heads of relevant central administrative agencies shall determine the recipients and scope of support by comprehensively considering the contribution of contract departments, etc. to demand for and supply of human resources in the semiconductor industry, their cooperation systems with business operators engaged in the semiconductor industry, etc. In such cases, priority may be given to supporting contract departments, etc. established at industrial educational institutions located outside the Seoul Metropolitan Area.
(4) Detailed matters concerning the criteria for selecting support recipients and the payment and management of subsidies, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (3), shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Trade and Industry.

CHAPTER VI SPECIAL ACCOUNT FOR STRENGTHENING SEMICONDUCTOR INDUSTRY COMPETITIVENESS

Article 34 (Establishment, Operation, and Management of the Special Account for Strengthening Semiconductor Industry Competitiveness)
(1) The Minister of Trade and Industry may request the head of a relevant central administrative agency to transfer projects related to the semiconductor industry belonging to accounts and funds operated and managed by that head to the Special Account for Strengthening Semiconductor Industry Competitiveness under Article 35(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Special Account").
(2) The Minister of Trade and Industry shall ensure that the Special Account is of an appropriate size, taking into consideration the master plan, semiconductor cluster development plans approved pursuant to the former part of Article 11(5) of the Act, with the exception of its subparagraphs, and expenditures under Article 36(2) of the Act.

Article 35 (Expenditures of the Special Account)
"Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 36(2)15 of the Act means the following projects:
1. Projects to support international cooperation and standardization activities related to the semiconductor industry;
2. Projects to support market development, such as exhibitions and academic conferences related to the semiconductor industry;
3. Projects to provide financial support and interest subsidies to SMEs and middle-standing enterprises related to the semiconductor industry;
4. Other projects the Committee deems urgently necessary to strengthen the competitiveness of the semiconductor industry.

Article 36 (Scope of Carryover of the Special Account Budget, etc.)
(1) The scope of the expenditure budget that may be carried over and used in the following fiscal year pursuant to Article 38(1) of the Act shall be determined by the Minister of Trade and Industry, in consultation with the Minister of Planning and Budget, within 20 percent of the total amount of each unit project's expenses for the relevant fiscal year. In such cases, the Minister of Trade and Industry shall hear in advance the opinions of the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and the heads of local governments.
(2) When submitting a statement of carryover pursuant to Article 38(2) of the Act, the Minister of Trade and Industry shall prepare it by classifying the details of the carryover as prescribed by the Minister of Planning and Budget.

Article 37 (Entrustment of Accounting Affairs, etc.)
(1) The Minister of Trade and Industry shall entrust the following affairs to the Korea Institute for Advancement of Technology under Article 38 of the Industrial Technology Innovation Promotion Act pursuant to Article 39(1) of the Act:
1. Affairs concerning the receipt of revenues set forth in the subparagraphs of Article 36(1) of the Act;
2. Affairs concerning the disbursement, settlement of accounts, asset management, etc. of budgets related to expenditures set forth in the subparagraphs of Article 36(2) of the Act.
(2) The Korea Institute for Advancement of Technology entrusted with the affairs set forth in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) shall report to the Minister of Trade and Industry on the operation and management of the entrusted affairs: monthly performance by the 15th day of the following month and annual performance by January 31 of the following year.
(3) The president of the Korea Institute for Advancement of Technology may prescribe matters necessary for the efficient performance of the entrusted affairs. In such cases, the president shall obtain approval from the Minister of Trade and Industry.

Article 38 (Other Special Accounts for Supporting the Semiconductor Industry)
"Special accounts prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 40, subparagraph 3 of the Act means the following special accounts:
1. The Special Account for Energy and Resources Projects under Article 3 of the Act on Special Accounts for Energy and Resources Projects;
2. The Special Account for Environmental Improvement under Article 45 of the Framework Act on Environmental Policy;
3. Other special accounts the Minister of Trade and Industry deems necessary to strengthen the competitiveness of the semiconductor industry and develop infrastructure and publicly notifies.

CHAPTER VII SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS AND PENALTY PROVISIONS

Article 39 (Procedures for Submission of Materials and Inspections)
(1) When ordering the submission of materials pursuant to Article 41(1) of the Act, the Minister of Trade and Industry or the head of a relevant administrative agency shall give written notice no later than seven days before the submission deadline.
(2) A public official intending to conduct an inspection pursuant to Article 41(2) of the Act shall notify the institution, corporation, or organization subject to inspection of an inspection plan containing the following matters no later than seven days before the entry and inspection: Provided, That this shall not apply where an urgent inspection is necessary or it is deemed that prior notification would prevent achievement of the purpose of the inspection:
1. The purpose, period, and place of the inspection;
2. The names and positions of the inspecting public officials;
3. The scope and details of the inspection.
(3) A public official who has conducted an inspection pursuant to paragraph (2) shall, after completing the inspection, notify the inspected institution, corporation, or organization of the results in writing.

Article 40 (Entrustment of Duties)
(1) The Minister of Trade and Industry may entrust the following duties to the Korea Institute for Advancement of Technology under Article 38 of the Industrial Technology Innovation Promotion Act, the Korea Planning and Evaluation Institute of Industrial Technology under Article 39 of that Act, or an institution, corporation, or organization related to the semiconductor industry pursuant to Article 42(2) of the Act:
1. Receipt of applications for regulatory improvement under Article 23(1) of the Act;
2. Receipt of applications for expedited processing of authorizations, permits, etc. under Article 27(1) of the Act;
3. Surveys and analyses concerning specialists at overseas universities, research institutes, and enterprises under Article 30(1) of the Act, and management and utilization of the results thereof;
4. Implementation of the matters set forth in the subparagraphs of Article 32(2) of the Act;
5. Receipt of applications for designation as training institutions under Article 33(1) of the Act and Article 31(2) of this Decree;
6. Receipt of applications for designation as universities specializing in semiconductors, etc. under Article 34(1) of the Act and Article 32(3) of this Decree;
7. Receipt of applications for support for the expenses of establishing and operating contract departments, etc. under Article 34(3) of the Act and Article 33(1) of this Decree.
(2) Where the Minister of Trade and Industry entrusts duties pursuant to paragraph (1), the Minister shall publicly notify the entrusted institution, corporation, or organization and the details of the entrusted duties.

Article 41 (Criteria for Imposition and Collection of Administrative Fines)
The criteria for imposing administrative fines under Article 44(1) of the Act shall be as specified in the Annex.

